  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe warm front pushed back north this morning bringing a round of \nrain across central IL. PWATs are expected to be around 1.8-2.1 \ninches this afternoon, which is very high for the month of October. \nThe 12z upper air sounding showed 1.77 inches for PWAT. The average \nPWAT for October 15th is around 0.6-0.7 inches based off the SPC \nsounding climatology. The good news is that our soils can handle \nthis rain since we have been so dry lately. The rain is welcomed. \nThe LPMM is showing some localized swaths of 1-3 inches of rain \nthrough tonight near the I-70 corridor on top of what has already \nfallen this morning. The rest of Central IL could see 0.25-0.75\" of \nrain during this time. Some areas under the heaviest rain has \nalready achieved the 1-3\" amounts. \n\nWhen the cold front advances east this afternoon/evening, a severe \nweather risk and additional rain will be accompanying it. With the \n1630z update, SPC has introduced a slight risk (level 2 of 5) to I-\n70 and south and a marginal risk still from about a Pittsfield to \nGibson City line and south. The environment looks supportive of \nstrong to severe thunderstorms if we can recover fully from the \nmorning stratus/fog and morning rainfall. Storm type looks like it \nmay begin as discrete, supercells that will evolve into a \nmulticell/QLCS mode as it exits to the east. Damaging winds and a \ntornado or two are the main hazards. Storms will move through from \n~20z-02z. After these storms exit to the east, additional light \nshowers could occur through Friday afternoon. After Friday \nafternoon, the forecast is expected to be dry through the extended \nperiod.\n\nThe cold front pushes off to our east early tomorrow morning. The \npressure gradient with the passing low pressure system will tighten \nbehind the cold front. Wind gusts around 30 mph will start late this \nevening and will continue for most of the day tomorrow. Wind gusts \ntomorrow will be around 30-40 mph, with the strongest winds possible \nwest of I-57. The HREF shows a 40-60% chance of gusts greater than \n35 mph west of I-57 in the morning. Winds should relax to closer to \n15-20 mph by sunset tomorrow.\n\nCooler, more fall like temperatures will be ushered in behind the \nfront for a few days. Highs tomorrow and Saturday will be in the \n50s, with lows in the mid to upper 30s. There is a chance for some \npatchy frost to develop in western IL Saturday morning, depending if \nthe wind will drop to near calm. Saturday morning may also have a \nrisk for a light freeze in some locations north of the IL River \nValley, especially in low spots. The HREF is showing a 40-60% chance \nof temperatures dropping below 32 degrees north of the IL River\nValley. Patchy frost is possible again Sunday morning. Beyond \nSunday morning, temperatures will begin to warm back up to low \n70s for highs on Monday, then to upper 70s by Wednesday next \nweek.",
